### Is there an existing issue for this?

- [x] I have searched the existing issues

### Describe the bug

When a request has a supported `Content-Encoding` (`gzip`, `br`, `deflate`, `zstd`), `RequestDecompressionMiddleware.InvokeCore` reads the size limit for the decompressed body **once**, before it calls `_next(context)`:


```csharp
var sizeLimit =
    context.GetEndpoint()?.Metadata?.GetMetadata<IRequestSizeLimitMetadata>()?.MaxRequestBodySize
        ?? context.Features.Get<IHttpMaxRequestBodySizeFeature>()?.MaxRequestBodySize;

context.Request.Body = new SizeLimitedStream(decompressionStream, sizeLimit, ...);
await _next(context);
```


([[src/Middleware/RequestDecompression/src/RequestDecompressionMiddleware.cs](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Middleware/RequestDecompression/src/RequestDecompressionMiddleware.cs)](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Middleware/RequestDecompression/src/RequestDecompressionMiddleware.cs))


[`[SizeLimitedStream](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Shared/SizeLimitedStream.cs)`](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/main/src/Shared/SizeLimitedStream.cs) keeps that value in a `readonly` field. It never checks the feature or the endpoint metadata again.


The docs say `IHttpMaxRequestBodySizeFeature.MaxRequestBodySize` can be changed per request (in middleware, filters, or handlers) until the body is read. They also say decompressed bodies are bounded by the endpoint or server limit:


- https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/fundamentals/middleware/request-decompression#request-size-limits
- https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/mvc/models/file-uploads#per-request-body-size-limits


The server (for example, Kestrel) applies the lowered value only to the **compressed** bytes it receives. The decompressed stream still uses the value captured earlier. So lowering the limit after `UseRequestDecompression()` has no effect on how much decompressed data the app reads.


The same thing happens with `IRequestSizeLimitMetadata` (for example, `[RequestSizeLimit]`) when `app.UseRouting()` is called explicitly **after** `app.UseRequestDecompression()`. In that order, no endpoint has been selected when the limit is captured. Routing applies the metadata limit to the feature later, which is too late. In the default `WebApplication` pipeline (no explicit `UseRouting()`), routing runs first and the limit works as expected.

### Expected Behavior

The decompressed body should be checked against the limit in effect when the body is read. If code lowers `MaxRequestBodySize` before reading, reading past the new limit should throw `BadHttpRequestException` with status 413. That is what already happens for uncompressed bodies and when routing runs before decompression.

### Steps To Reproduce

Standalone Kestrel app (`Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web`):


```csharp
using System.IO.Compression;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Metadata;

var uncompressed = new byte[2 * 1024 * 1024]; // 2 MB of zeros
byte[] compressed;
using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
{
    using (var gz = new GZipStream(ms, CompressionLevel.Optimal, leaveOpen: true)) { gz.Write(uncompressed); }
    compressed = ms.ToArray(); // 2,067 bytes
}

async Task Run(string name, bool decompression, bool routingAfter, bool lowerInHandler, bool metadata, bool gzip)
{
    var builder = WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder();
    builder.WebHost.UseKestrel().UseUrls("http://127.0.0.1:0");
    builder.Services.AddRequestDecompression();
    var app = builder.Build();
    if (!routingAfter) app.UseRouting();
    if (decompression) app.UseRequestDecompression();
    if (routingAfter) app.UseRouting();
    var ep = app.MapPost("/", async (HttpContext ctx) =>
    {
        if (lowerInHandler) ctx.Features.Get<IHttpMaxRequestBodySizeFeature>()!.MaxRequestBodySize = 4096;
        using var ms = new MemoryStream();
        try { await ctx.Request.Body.CopyToAsync(ms); }
        catch (BadHttpRequestException ex) { return Results.Text(ex.Message, statusCode: ex.StatusCode); }
        return Results.Text($"read={ms.Length}");
    });
    if (metadata) ep.WithMetadata(new Limit());
    await app.StartAsync();
    using var client = new HttpClient();
    var content = new ByteArrayContent(gzip ? compressed : uncompressed);
    if (gzip) content.Headers.ContentEncoding.Add("gzip");
    var resp = await client.PostAsync(app.Urls.First(), content);
    Console.WriteLine($"{name}: {(int)resp.StatusCode} {await resp.Content.ReadAsStringAsync()}");
    await app.StopAsync();
}

await Run("A uncompressed, no decompression, handler lowers to 4096", false, false, true, false, false);
await Run("B gzip, decompression, handler lowers to 4096",          true,  false, true, false, true);
await Run("C gzip, UseRouting BEFORE decompression, metadata 4096", true,  false, false, true, true);
await Run("D gzip, UseRouting AFTER decompression, metadata 4096",  true,  true,  false, true, true);

class Limit : IRequestSizeLimitMetadata { public long? MaxRequestBodySize => 4096; }
```


Output:


```
A uncompressed, no decompression, handler lowers to 4096: 413 Request body too large. The max request body size is 4096 bytes.
B gzip, decompression, handler lowers to 4096: 200 read=2097152
C gzip, UseRouting BEFORE decompression, metadata 4096: 413 The decompressed request body is larger than the request body size limit 4096.
D gzip, UseRouting AFTER decompression, metadata 4096: 200 read=2097152
```


- **A** shows that Kestrel applies a limit lowered in the handler.
- **C** shows that the decompression middleware applies a limit that already exists when it runs.
- **B** and **D** show the bug. The limit drops to 4,096 before the body is read, the 2,067-byte compressed request passes Kestrel's check, and the endpoint reads all 2,097,152 decompressed bytes with `200 OK` instead of 413.

### Exceptions (if any)

None. That is the problem: the expected `BadHttpRequestException` (413, "The decompressed request body is larger than the request body size limit 4096.") is never thrown in cases B and D.

### .NET Version

Reproduced with Kestrel on `Microsoft.AspNetCore.App` 11.0.0-rc.1.26420.103. The middleware has read the limit this way since it was added in .NET 7.

### Anything else?

Possible fix: read the limit when the body is read, not when the middleware runs. For example, `SizeLimitedStream` (or a wrapper used only for decompression) could take a `Func<long?>` or the `HttpContext`, and on the first read check endpoint metadata first and then `IHttpMaxRequestBodySizeFeature`. This matches how servers treat the feature: it can be changed until reading starts and is read-only afterwards.


Related code:


- `src/Middleware/RequestDecompression/src/RequestDecompressionMiddleware.cs` (`InvokeCore`)
- `src/Shared/SizeLimitedStream.cs`

## Summary
HTTP/3 works with Kestrel/YARP when the TLS certificate is configured directly on the endpoint, but fails with an ALPN negotiation error when equivalent SNI configuration is enabled.

> Note: `example.com` is used as a placeholder. Reproduced with real domains and valid X.509 certificates.

## Environment
- .NET SDK: 11.0.100
- Runtime: Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 11.0.0
- OS: Windows 11 (10.0.26300)
- Also observed on Ubuntu 24.04
- YARP: 2.3.0

## Expected behavior
An HTTP/3 request to `https://example.com` succeeds both with and without SNI configuration.

## Actual behavior
With SNI enabled, HTTP/3-only clients fail with:
`System.Net.Http.HttpRequestException: Application layer protocol negotiation error was encountered (no_application_protocol)`

HTTP/2 continues to work.

### FYI
- The issue is reproducible with a standalone Kestrel (plus SNI config) application. YARP is not required to reproduce the problem.
- The same behavior was observed on both Windows 11 and Ubuntu 24.04.

## Impact on YARP

YARP is commonly used as a reverse proxy to host multiple websites and domains on a single server or VPS, sharing the same public IP address and HTTPS port (443).

In this scenario, SNI is particularly important because it allows the server to select the appropriate TLS certificate based on the requested hostname, without requiring a separate IP address or port for each domain.

Since HTTP/3 is increasingly relevant for modern web applications, the inability to combine SNI with HTTP/3 could be a significant limitation for YARP deployments hosting multiple domains.

Although the issue is reproducible in a standalone Kestrel application and is therefore not specific to YARP, it directly affects a common YARP deployment scenario.

## Minimal repro

`Program.cs` includes:
- `AppContext.SetSwitch("System.Net.SocketsHttpHandler.Http3Support", true);`
- `builder.WebHost.UseQuic(...);`

### YARP Case A: Works (no SNI)
```
"Kestrel": {
  "Endpoints": {
    "Https": {
      "Url": "https://*:443",
      "Protocols": "Http1AndHttp2AndHttp3",
      "SslProtocols": [ "Tls13" ],
      "Certificate": { "Path": "cert.pfx", "Password": "..." }
    }
  }
}
```

### YARP Case B: Fails (with SNI)
```
"Kestrel": {
  "Endpoints": {
    "HttpsSni": {
      "Url": "https://*:443",
      "Protocols": "Http1AndHttp2AndHttp3",
      "SslProtocols": [ "Tls13" ],
      "Certificate": { "Path": "cert.pfx", "Password": "..." },
      "Sni": {
        "example.com": {
          "Protocols": "Http1AndHttp2AndHttp3",
          "SslProtocols": [ "Tls13" ],
          "Certificate": { "Path": "cert.pfx", "Password": "..." }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Test client (HTTP/3 only)
```
AppContext.SetSwitch("System.Net.SocketsHttpHandler.Http3Support", true);

using var client = new HttpClient(new SocketsHttpHandler { UseProxy = false })
{
    DefaultRequestVersion = HttpVersion.Version30,
    DefaultVersionPolicy = HttpVersionPolicy.RequestVersionExact
};

await client.GetAsync("https://example.com/appsettings.json");
```

